ARTICLES = {
"marxists-internet-archive": {"non_row": "marxists-internet-archive",
 "kernel": "The Marxists Internet Archive (marxists.org) is an all-volunteer, non-profit public library of Marxist writings and writings relevant to the understanding of Marxism, established in its present form in 1998 from a collection begun in 1990. Its unit is the document, transcribed by a volunteer and filed in a language section, and it holds over 400,000 documents in 83 languages (April 2026).",
 "kernel_ko": [1, 3, 4, 15],
 "sections": [
  ("What it is", ["It describes itself as \"an all-volunteer, non-profit public library, established in its present form in 1998\", and its home page as \"The most complete library of Marxism\". Its aim is \"any and all writings which are Marxist or relevant to the understanding of Marxism and can be lawfully published\", and it holds the writings of close to 1000 authors over some 200 years. Beside its author archives it keeps subject sections, a reference Encyclopedia of Marxism, and, under its History path, the Encyclopedia of anti-Revisionism On-Line."]),
  ("History", ["The collection began in 1990, when a person known only as Zodiac began transcribing Marx and Engels, starting with the Communist Manifesto. As it opened to other currents, Zodiac feared a \"slippery slope\" toward sectarianism and in early 1998 decided that all writers other than Marx and Engels would be removed. In July 1998 the volunteers who had done the transcribing created marxists.org from Marx.org's files.",
               "In late April 2014 Lawrence & Wishart revoked permission for their English Marx/Engels Collected Works to be reprinted in part on the archive and asked for their deletion under threat of litigation. The archive complied, and a petition against the decision gathered more than 4,500 supporters."]),
  ("How it is held", ["A steering committee of all active volunteers decides the categorization of writers, the bylaws and the finances; 76 active volunteers from several dozen countries were counted in July 2025. It is incorporated in California as a 501(c)(3) non-profit, and its material, unless noted otherwise, is public domain. What it holds is bounded \"within the limits imposed by the availability of a willing volunteer to transcribe the works and the impact of intellectual property laws\". Its texts are volunteer transcriptions, with no editorial apparatus stated."]),
  ("Whose record, who holds it, the losing side", ["Its Charter binds it to seven tenets, among them \"(5) We will always remain politically independent; (6) We do not ‘judge’ writers\". In 2007 it ended \"dividing all writers into Marxists and non-Marxists\", and it filters out no writer's views and endorses none of them. It names no section for splits or expelled tendencies.",
               "The archive reads it as an independent holder whose Charter and 2007 decision keep a writer in the record whatever verdict a party passed: \"the holder condition of #1725 without a route: the text stays available to be quoted, and what is kept depends on a volunteer willing to transcribe it\". At its name on 31 October 2026, Google AI Mode gave \"Over 180,000 documents across 80+ languages\", a count from an earlier state of the site, and left out the sixth tenet and the 2007 decision, which the Capture Registry reads as \"what keeps the losing side of every split on its shelves\"."])],
 "ko": [[[1, 2, 6, 3]], [[4, 5], [24]], [[13, 14, 17, 18]], [[7, 8], [11, 29, 30]]]},

"mega-digital": {"non_row": "megadigital",
 "kernel": "MEGAdigital is the online division of the Marx-Engels-Gesamtausgabe (MEGA), the historical-critical edition of all the published works, manuscripts, drafts and correspondence of Marx and Engels, an Academy project of the Berlin-Brandenburgische Akademie der Wissenschaften published by the Internationale Marx-Engels-Stiftung (IMES). Its unit is a volume in one of four divisions.",
 "kernel_ko": [1, 3],
 "sections": [
  ("What it is", ["The MEGA reproduces all the writings of Marx and Engels \"in their entirety and in their original languages, meticulously documenting all corrections, deletions, and variations in the manuscripts\", and \"presents the texts without interpretation\". Its four divisions are \"I. Works, Articles, and Drafts; II. Das Kapital and its preliminary studies; III. Correspondence; and IV. Excerpts, Notebooks, and Marginalia\". Online are the edited texts of ten volumes of the second division, the letters from 1866, and most of the excerpts and notes of the fourth; the first division appears in print only. The site is licensed CC BY-SA 4.0."]),
  ("History", ["The first edition, MEGA¹, began in the 1920s under David Riazanov at the Marx–Engels Institute in Moscow; Riazanov was removed in 1931, and the edition was discontinued in 1941 after 12 volumes. MEGA², a joint venture of the Institutes for Marxism-Leninism in East Berlin and Moscow, issued its first volume in 1975, its editing \"influenced by the official ideology of the ruling communist parties\".",
               "After 1989 the edition continued under the IMES, founded in 1990 on the initiative of the International Institute of Social History to continue it \"on a purely scientific and politically independent basis\", with the BBAW coordinating. As of 2026, 62 of 114 planned volumes were published; the latest listed are IV/24, online in August 2026, and I/9, in print in December 2025."]),
  ("Custody and what the edition found", ["In the archive's reading, the edition's beginnings were correctives to custodians: Kautsky \"extensively rearranged Marx's notebooks, deleted passages he considered unimportant, and imposed chapter divisions\", deletions found only when MEGA² printed the full text, and MEGA² was \"designed partly as a corrective to prior Soviet collections\". The International Institute of Social History had acquired most of Marx's original manuscripts from the SPD in 1938 and opened its archives to MEGA². Volume I/5 (2017) established the fragmentary character of The German Ideology."]),
  ("Whose record, who holds it, the losing side", ["It keeps Marx's and Engels's own drafts, excerpts and correspondence, letters to them from others included, held by an Academy in the federally and state-funded Akademienprogramm, with an independent foundation overseeing the edition. It names no apparatus for rival tendencies, and its losing side is not recorded as such. The archive reads a complete edition of drafts, excerpts and letters as the record of Marx's self-revision, \"kept with the stages the published text superseded\".",
               "At the bare name \"MEGAdigital\", Google's AI Overview of 31 October 2026 composed Mega Digital JSC, a marketing agency. The Capture Registry finds \"No sentence names Marx, Engels, the MEGA or the Berlin-Brandenburg Academy\", and gives the innocent reading first: the Overview resolves a bare compound name \"to the company that most uses it\"."])],
 "ko": [[[13, 15, 16, 26]], [[5, 6], [7, 4, 17]], [[8, 9, 20]], [[23, 24, 4, 3], [27]]]},

"bannedthought": {"non_row": "bannedthought",
 "kernel": "BannedThought.net is a web site that publicizes the suppression of progressive ideas and posts documents and publications suppressed elsewhere, chiefly those of revolutionary and Maoist parties and movements, filed by country and by party. It states that it is independent and unaffiliated with any political party, and names no operator.",
 "kernel_ko": [1, 2, 4, 5],
 "sections": [
  ("What it is", ["It exists, on its home page, \"to struggle against the fascist-like suppression of progressive ideas and thought by reactionary governments and their agents anywhere in the world\". Its sections run from Afghanistan to \"Yugoslavia and Balkans Region\", about fifty in all, with international associations; it gives no span or count, and its latest dated item is of 5 May 2022. For the Communist Party of India (Maoist) it holds documents, statements, an information bulletin and the theoretical journal People’s War; for China, Mao's writings, the revolution to 1949 and the Cultural Revolution; and the papers of CCOMPOSA, RIM, the ICL and the Comintern."]),
  ("How it works", ["Readers report banned or suppressed documents \"so that we may inform others of this and possibly post them on this site\", and country pages are added \"as becomes necessary\". It states that \"because of our own language deficiencies, we are usually only posting items in English\", and holds documents in Gondi and Telugu and magazines in Hindi and Telugu. It names its sections in its own terms, such as \"U.S.S.R. (Socialist & Revisionist Periods)\". The site is itself banned in India, by its own account, and posts the censorship agency's letter with an addendum of May 5, 2022."]),
  ("Use and limits", ["Scholarship draws on it: a 2021 article on the Philippine left takes Communist Party of the Philippines texts of 1993 and 2016 from it, and sixteen Wikipedia articles cite documents it hosts. Its record cannot settle whether its holdings for any party are complete, the provenance and date of each posted document, or \"the documents of tendencies the site does not count as progressive or suppressed\"."]),
  ("Whose record, who holds it, the losing side", ["Rival parties of one tradition stand side by side: for Nepal the Communist Party of Nepal (Revolutionary Maoist) and the Communist Party of Nepal (Maoist Centre), each with its own documents and statements, and for India the CPI(M-L) \"[Original Party formed in 1969]\" beside the People’s War party and others. The archive reads the site as \"A holder independent of every party whose documents it posts\", keeping both sides of the Nepal split \"in their own words\", its record of internal division \"a by-product\" of its question of suppression by states.",
               "At its name on 31 October 2026, the Overview gave its purpose, holdings and bans from the site and folded the splits into \"broader Marxist-Leninist-Maoist movements\"; the Capture Registry records \"THE INDEPENDENCE NOT CARRIED\", with no holder named."])],
 "ko": [[[1, 4, 5]], [[14, 6, 11]], [[18, 19, 16]], [[7, 9], [22, 23]]]},

"erol": {"non_row": "erol",
 "kernel": "The Encyclopedia of anti-Revisionism On-Line (EROL) is an online encyclopedia of information on, and primary documents from, anti-revisionist movements, organizations and parties, by country, served by the Marxists Internet Archive and kept by an EROL Administrator. Its unit is a primary document filed by country and by period subsection, and its record runs from 1946.",
 "kernel_ko": [1, 2, 4, 12],
 "sections": [
  ("What it is", ["Its definition page states its purpose: \"The goal of the Encyclopedia of Anti-Revisionism is to document this trend\", signed \"Paul C., 2009\". The trend developed in the pro-Soviet Communist movement after the Second World War, grew at the crisis of the CPSU's 20th Congress in 1956, criticized the official parties \"from the left\", made the defence of Stalin its hallmark, expanded with the Sino-Soviet split and the Cultural Revolution, and declined after Mao's death. The tendency it records is itself a minority defined against \"revisionism\"."]),
  ("What it holds", ["Its contents open with a definition and representative materials from China and Albania, file the United States by three waves of anti-revisionism from 1946 to 1970 and by the New Communist Movement from its origins in 1969, and give 26 country sections in all, Canada and the United Kingdom among them; document counts are not stated. A periodicals page lists the movements' papers by country, from Challenge/Desafio and Workers’ Advocate (1969-93) in the United States to The Forge and In Struggle in Canada, and runs past 1983 to The Line of March (\"begun 2011\")."]),
  ("How it is held", ["Its licence is its host's, public domain unless noted otherwise; its gathering method and its Administrator are not described. Harvard's catalogue record dates its materials \"1946-1983\" and states that it \"may reproduce copyright-protected content without license or permissions, contain re-typed content that cannot be verified for accuracy or completeness, and/or omit facts of publication or archival attribution\"."]),
  ("Whose record, who holds it, the losing side", ["Its structure is organized around splits and defeat. The New Communist Movement sections are titled \"Birth of the Anti-Dogmatist Trend, 1976-1977\", \"Crises, Splits and More New Parties, 1977-1980\", \"Anti-Dogmatists Unable to Unite, 1978-1980\" and \"Collapse and Aftermath\"; Canada runs to \"Crisis and Collapse, 1982-1983\", and the United Kingdom to \"Ebbing Tide: Decline and Dissolution\". Periodicals are named by the splits that made them, the Chicago Workers’ Voice of \"former members of the Marxist-Leninist Party\".",
               "The archive reads it as \"An organ that keeps a defeated tendency's record in that tendency's own documents, sectioned by its splits and its collapse, so each faction of a split stays stated after the organization that housed it ended\". #1725 draws its SDS case from a document in its New Communist Movement section, the account of the 1969 expulsion of Progressive Labor. At its name the Overview gave the encyclopedia's subject and range with its host named, and left out its periodization by splits, unity attempts and collapse."])],
 "ko": [[[5, 6, 7, 8]], [[9, 11, 14, 16]], [[18, 21, 19, 20]], [[10, 11, 13, 15], [23, 27, 29, 30]]]},

"banmarchive": {"non_row": "amiel-melburn-trust-archive",
 "kernel": "The Amiel Melburn Trust Archive (banmarchive.org.uk) is an indexed and searchable online database of British socialist and radical writings, held by the Barry Amiel and Norman Melburn Trust, a registered charity founded in 1980. Its unit is a digitized issue: 13 publications and 335 issues, from The Reasoner (1956) to New University (2024).",
 "kernel_ko": [1, 3, 6, 11, 13],
 "sections": [
  ("What it is", ["It holds thirteen publications, from The Reasoner (3 issues, 1956) and the Socialist History Society Pamphlets (38 issues, 1956–1976) through Marxism Today (145 issues, 1978–1998) to New University (13 issues, 1960–2024), more than 2,500 articles in all, most in PDF, which preserves the original look and typesetting. Its other titles include Red Rag, Race & Class and Black Dwarf. It is defined \"primarily as an educational resource\" whose material \"is meant to be relatively ecumenical in political terms\", drawn mostly from hard copies already out of print."]),
  ("Who holds it", ["It belongs to the Barry Amiel and Norman Melburn Trust, Registered Charity No. 281239. The Trust was founded in 1980 by Norman Melburn and named for his friend and fellow Marxist, the lawyer Barry Amiel. Its objectives are to advance public education \"in all aspects of the philosophy of Marxism, the history of socialism, and the working class movement\", and it gives project grants of up to £15,750, the maintenance of libraries and archive material among the projects funded before. Common Knowledge built the present site in 2021 on Django and Wagtail, with a search that indexes the PDFs on upload, and released its source code."]),
  ("How it works", ["It is searched by word and by exact phrase, with an advanced search and a filter by publication. Its About page sets use \"exclusively for educational purposes, non-commercial research and private study\" under CC BY-NC-ND 4.0, with a takedown procedure under which every decision is recorded."]),
  ("Whose record, who holds it, the losing side", ["Its first titles exist because a party ordered one closed. When E.P. Thompson and John Saville were instructed by the leadership of the Communist Party of Great Britain to close The Reasoner, which had criticized what they saw as the party's dogmatism, they refused. Suspended, they resigned over the Soviet invasion of Hungary and relaunched as the New Reasoner, whose ten issues run from Summer 1957 to Autumn 1959 and which later merged with the Universities and Left Review to become New Left Review. The archive holds both runs whole.",
               "The archive reads the case by #1725's three terms: \"This holds the organ a defeated critique built for itself\", its holder \"a charitable trust with standing independent of the party criticized\", and its New Reasoner page \"states the route ran through the merger into New Left Review\". It holds no party records, so the Communist Party leadership's own account of the 1956 dispute lies outside it. At its name the Overview gave the archive and its Trust at their grain and kept the year 1956 \"without the event\"."])],
 "ko": [[[11, 12, 13, 22]], [[3, 6, 7, 9, 25]], [[23, 24]], [[14, 15, 16, 17], [19, 20, 21, 28]]]},

"libcom-library": {"non_row": "libcom-collections",
 "kernel": "libcom.org is a libertarian communist website whose Collections index holds texts, archives of publications, reading guides, blogs and galleries, filed under tags for strikes, movements and writers. Launched in 2003 as enrager.net and renamed in 2005, it states that it is independent of all trade unions and political parties and is funded by its volunteer administrators and its users.",
 "kernel_ko": [1, 3, 6, 19],
 "sections": [
  ("What it is", ["The site addresses \"all people who wish to fight to improve their lives, their communities and their working conditions\" and wants to \"learn from successes and failures of the past\"; its name abbreviates \"libertarian communism\". It names its influences as anarchist-communism, anarcho-syndicalism, Autonomist Marxism, black liberation, council communism, feminism and ultra-left Marxism. Its introduction of 2006 counted \"a constantly growing archive of over 20,000 articles contributed by our 10,000+ users\"."]),
  ("What it holds", ["Its tags run from the 1926 general strike, the 1934 Minneapolis Teamsters strike and the 8-hour day through the CNT, the Flint sit-down strike and France 1968 to the Black Lives Matter movement and the Grenfell fire, with writers among them: Amadeo Bordiga, Anton Pannekoek, CLR James, Emma Goldman, Gilles Dauvé, Guy Debord. Its Archives hold \"Anarchist, communist, libertarian socialist, and ultra-left publications, journals, magazines, newspapers, bulletins, and pamphlets\", Aufheben, Black Flag, Endnotes, Internationale Situationniste and Kronstadt Izvestia among them, with organisations' papers such as the Solidarity Federation's Direct Action. Its Galleries keep posters, covers and photographs, the covers of Class War from 1983 to 2017 among them."]),
  ("What its record leaves open", ["Its About addresses serve the site's not-found page, so the holder, licence and method of the collections are unstated, and the index's count is cut off at \"Load More\". Its record answers which subjects, strikes, organisations and writers the collections are tagged under, and leaves open how many texts they hold and how texts are selected, transcribed or edited."]),
  ("Whose record, who holds it, the losing side", ["It keeps writings and accounts of anarchism, strikes and social movements, and states no policy on the losing side; its tags name writers of tendencies apart from the party traditions. The Capture Registry reads the names as currents: \"Pannekoek's council communists and Bordiga's left were the currents the Communist International expelled; libcom keeps their texts\". The archive reads the index as \"holding writers of anti-party and left-communist tendencies next to strike histories, which would make it an organ for positions the parties lost\", a reading that \"waits on its About page\".",
               "#1725 takes from it Lotta Continua's Rimini congress of 1976, where the general secretary said \"I was wrong\". At its name the Overview gave \"A faithful map of the site's sections\" from six libcom.org cards, flattened \"the losing currents of the Third International into a list of figures\", and named no holder."])],
 "ko": [[[2, 3, 15, 20]], [[7, 8, 10, 11, 13]], [[18, 9, 25]], [[16, 17], [23, 26, 27]]]},

"irish-left-archive": {"non_row": "irish-left-archive",
 "kernel": "The Irish Left Archive is an online archive of materials relating to Irish left politics, freely accessible and easy to download and reproduce, initiated in 2007 by the Cedar Lounge Revolution and maintained by its co-curators Ciarán Swan and Aonghus Storey. Its unit is a dated document attributed to the organisation that issued it: 1231 documents from 436 organisations on 31 October 2026.",
 "kernel_ko": [1, 3, 5, 10],
 "sections": [
  ("What it is", ["It was intended for \"documents, leaflets, pamphlets, posters and newspapers from the latter half of the 20th century placed online in PDF format\", and its scope is \"materials from all on the left\", from socialist and Marxist to anarchist, feminist, trade union and \"left and progressive Unionist voices\". In 2013, at about 300 documents, it was given a database website. Beside its 1231 documents it lists 47 internationals, 217 publications and 875 people, an interactive timeline of organisations \"from 1900 to the present\", and collections such as the Wood Quay protests and the Hunger Strikes of 1980/1."]),
  ("How it works", ["It takes \"submissions of any materials that seem relevant to the scope of the archive, either in physical or digital form\", adds them \"on a weekly basis\" after \"scanning, reading and writing the accompanying commentaries\", and invites participants' reminiscences, which its comments carry. Documents continue to be posted to the Cedar Lounge Revolution, where the discussions have supplied context and corrections. Its podcast, hosted by the two co-curators, reached episode 72 on 30 October 2026."]),
  ("How it is held", ["It \"is not linked to any political party, and no judgement is made, beyond points regarding quality of production, on these documents in the accompanying texts\". Its Copyright page holds that the documents were \"intended for widespread dissemination\", that it modifies them only to scan and digitize them, and that copyright stays \"with the original authors\"; it identifies \"the organisation and the named contributors for each document\". Its curators state that \"large gaps still remain both chronologically and in terms of individual groups or formations\"."]),
  ("Whose record, who holds it, the losing side", ["Rival organisations' documents stand side by side under their issuers' names, the Communist Party of Ireland's Irish Socialist of 1973 beside the Socialist Workers' Party's Socialist Worker of 2001, and small and splinter organisations' records are kept current. Splits and expulsions are not described as a category. The archive reads it \"as an organ in #1725's sense at the scale of a national left: it keeps defeated and splinter organisations' documents under their own names next to their rivals', with a timeline of the organisations and participants' own accounts, so a position can be read later in its own words\".",
               "At its name the Overview gave its holdings, timeline, personal accounts and podcast, and left out its origin: \"The archive's founding by a left blog's community, which is who holds the record, is absent\"."])],
 "ko": [[[1, 9, 4, 10, 11, 20]], [[22, 21, 3, 23]], [[6, 8, 10]], [[12, 17], [28, 29]]]},

"iish": {"non_row": "iish",
 "kernel": "The International Institute of Social History (IISH) in Amsterdam is an archive and research institute of social history and the history of labour and social movements, officially established in 1935 by Nicolaas Posthumus and an institute of the Royal Netherlands Academy of Arts and Sciences. It holds more than 5,000 archival collections and thousands of datasets on socio-economic history.",
 "kernel_ko": [1, 4, 16, 13, 25],
 "sections": [
  ("What it is", ["Its archives, publications and audiovisual collections \"cover more than 50 kilometres\" and give information \"on organisations and persons in the field of labour and social and emancipatory movements\". About ten percent of its collections, nearly 500 archives, are online. It calls itself \"one of the world's leading data centres on socio-economic history\", with datasets on demography, wages, life courses, inequality, prices and labour relations, and it organizes the European Social Science History Conference. Since 1989 it has stood in an old warehouse at Cruquiusweg 31."]),
  ("Rescue and custody", ["It was founded in the 1930s, when, after Hitler's seizure of power, many documents and archives of opposition movements were threatened with loss. Brought to safety in Amsterdam were the archive of the German socialist movement, the manuscripts of Marx and Engels included, the archives of revolutionaries at risk from Stalin, and those of the Spanish trade unions. Before the German invasion of May 1940 the most valuable archives went to London; the Reichsleiter Rosenberg Taskforce took most of the rest to Nazi Germany between June 1942 and September 1944, and most were found in Hannover in 1946, other parts in Moscow archives in 1991."]),
  ("What it holds", ["Its papers include institutional collections of Amnesty International, the CNT and the FAI, the Russian Socialist Revolutionary Party and the Socialist International, and the personal papers of Emma Goldman, Karl Marx, Max Nettlau, Lev Trotsky, Karl Kautsky and Sylvia Pankhurst; it has been called \"the foremost repository of anarchist documents in the world\". Marx's archive is designated a UNESCO World Heritage Site and fully digitized in its catalogue."]),
  ("Who holds it", ["Its collections are the property of, or on standing loan to, the independent IISH Foundation. The reading room is free and open to everyone, and nothing is lent, because \"The material at the IISH must be available to everyone\". Some archives need the donor's or lender's permission for consultation, scans or publication, and the institute mediates in obtaining it."]),
  ("The losing side", ["Its mission is stated as \"preserving the heritage of often oppressed social movements\"; it states no policy on the losing side and describes no tendency's papers as such. The archive reads it as \"a holder in #1725's sense: an academy institute that keeps the records of organisations and persons of labour and emancipatory movements outside the bodies that produced them\", and the Capture Registry reads its papers as one custody for both sides of the First International's split: \"The parties to the First International's 1872 expulsion (#1725) are held by one institution\"."])],
 "ko": [[[2, 13, 25, 27]], [[5, 7, 8]], [[11, 12, 14]], [[17, 22, 23]], [[21, 19, 15]]]},

"reuther-library": {"non_row": "reuther-library",
 "kernel": "The Walter P. Reuther Library, Archives of Labor and Urban Affairs, at Wayne State University in Detroit, holds the collections of numerous unions and labor-related organizations and calls itself \"the largest labor archives in North America\". Established in 1960 as the Labor History Archives, it is held by the university; its unit is the collection with its finding aid.",
 "kernel_ko": [1, 6, 8, 25],
 "sections": [
  ("What it is", ["It was \"established as the Labor History Archives at Wayne State University in 1960, with the goal of collecting and preserving original source materials relating to the development of the American labor movement\", its collections growing for over thirty years under director Philip P. Mason. Its extent is given as over 75,000 linear feet in manuscript, photographic, oral-history, audio and film media. It is also the home of the Wayne State University Archives, established in 1958."]),
  ("Whose record it keeps", ["It serves as the official archival repository for eleven unions, the Industrial Workers of the World, the UAW and the United Farm Workers among them; in August 2026 it opened an addition to the UAW Education Department Records. Beyond the unions' own records it holds material of the Coalition of Labor Union Women and the Coalition of Black Trade Unionists, personal papers of activists such as James and Grace Lee Boggs and Raya Dunayevskaya, oral histories of union leaders and rank-and-file members, and collections on the political and community life of Detroit, the civil rights movement and women's struggles in the workplace."]),
  ("Who holds it", ["Wayne State University operates and owns it. Its 1975 building \"was constructed with funds given to Wayne State University by the United Automobile, Aerospace, and Agricultural Implement Workers of America, and through a supplementary grant from the Department of Health, Education, and Welfare\", and a later UAW gift funded the Leonard Woodcock Wing, completed in 1991. Its collections are \"freely open to the public. Appointments are not required\", and are found through finding aids in ArchivesSpace, Digital Collections and the Library Catalog. Its site reads \"All rights reserved\"."]),
  ("The losing side", ["Whether it keeps the losing side is not recorded: whether a defeated caucus's papers are held beside an executive's \"is a question for its finding aids, unread here\". The archive reads it as \"a university holder whose building was funded by the union whose records it keeps\", the holder of whom #1725 asks \"standing independent of the executive\". At its full name the Overview advised a research appointment where the library's page says \"Appointments are not required\", and gave the years of the building and wing without the gift."])],
 "ko": [[[6, 2, 15]], [[10, 11, 13, 14]], [[8, 7, 24, 25, 26]], [[16, 9, 30]]]},

"cornell-labor-action-tracker": {"non_row": "labor-action-tracker",
 "kernel": "The Labor Action Tracker is a database of strike and labor protest activity across the United States, with a map, run by Cornell University's ILR School and the University of Illinois' LER School and directed by Johnnie Kallas. Public since 1 May 2021 as the Cornell ILR Labor Action Tracker and renamed in January 2024, it codes strikes and labor protests from public sources.",
 "kernel_ko": [1, 2, 4, 14],
 "sections": [
  ("What it is", ["Its stated aim is \"a comprehensive database of strike and labor protest activity across the United States to better inform and support labor movement activists, policymakers, and scholars\". The project originated at the ILR School in late 2020, and it thanks the staff of the China Labour Bulletin \"for inspiring and encouraging us to develop this project\". Its annual report sets it against the federal count: since funding cuts in the early 1980s, the Bureau of Labor Statistics \"has only documented work stoppages involving 1,000 or more workers that last at least an entire shift\"."]),
  ("How it works", ["Information \"is from public sources and manually inputted into our tracker\" under a methodology of definitions, protocols and variables. Its sources include existing work stoppage databases, news articles and social media posts, with verification protocols \"to ensure that a strike did in fact occur\" and variables of \"size, duration, industry, and demands\". Strikes are documented from late 2020 and protests from early 2021, the strike database \"most accurate as of 2021\". Its map counts locations, so \"a single strike at five locations will appear five times\", and for protests it aims at \"a general understanding of labor protest activity\" short of a complete count. A spreadsheet of the data is had by email from the director."]),
  ("What it found", ["Its Annual Report 2025 documented \"303 work stoppages (298 strikes and five lockouts) involving approximately 290,000 workers\" and \"approximately 4,150,000 strike days\". More strikes occurred in Accommodation and Food Service than in any other industry, the West held \"77.1% of all workers on strike\", and \"95.5%\" of unionized strikes were authorized by a union."]),
  ("The losing side", ["It keeps the record of strikes and labor protests in the United States as reported in public sources, held by a university research project outside the unions it counts, and records nothing of the losing side. The archive reads it as recording \"that a strike happened\" and keeping \"nothing of the arguments inside the movement over whether or how to strike\"; beside that reading stands the report's count of unionized strikes in 2025, \"2.9% that were unauthorized\". At its name the Overview set the tracker against the federal threshold and left its debt to the China Labour Bulletin uncarried."])],
 "ko": [[[1, 4, 6, 7]], [[14, 15, 16, 18]], [[20, 21]], [[22, 23, 24, 27]]]},

"crowd-counting-consortium": {"non_row": "crowd-counting-consortium",
 "kernel": "The Crowd Counting Consortium is a public interest and scholarly project of Harvard Kennedy School and the University of Connecticut that documents protests and demonstrations in the United States, co-directed by Erica Chenoweth and Jeremy Pressman. Begun in 2017 with an estimate of the Women’s March on Washington, it publishes its event data monthly on the Harvard Dataverse.",
 "kernel_ko": [1, 2, 5, 6, 14],
 "sections": [
  ("What it is", ["It \"collects publicly available data on political protest events reported in the United States, including rallies, marches, demonstrations, and acts of civil disobedience\", riots included, and does not count crowds at meetings, teach-ins, panel discussions, fundraisers or town halls. Its goal is \"to make the aggregate data on crowd numbers publicly available for each event\". It is part of the Nonviolent Action Lab at the Ash Center and is funded in part by foundations and university centres, the MacArthur Foundation among them."]),
  ("How it works", ["It has worked with countlove.org, \"a volunteer group that developed a webcrawler to identify protests and demonstrations on a daily basis\". \"We only post records that we can confirm and verify through fact-checking\", a submission must carry \"a source that is publicly verifiable\", and \"We will never post, release, or share identifying information that has not already been reported in the public domain.\" Its data appear in three phases, 2017–2020, 2021–2024 and 2025 to the present, with a compiled version on GitHub updated weekly and an interactive dashboard."]),
  ("What it has counted", ["On 29 August 2024 it wrote \"Since 2017, we've tallied 200,000 protests and rallies in the US...and counting.\" It recorded nearly 12,400 pro-Palestine and over 2,000 pro-Israel protests from 7 October 2023 to 7 June 2024. Its data have been used to show the 2020 Black Lives Matter uprising \"overwhelmingly peaceful\" and the No Kings protests of June and October 2025 among the largest single-day demonstrations in US history, and its team's work has more than 600 scholarly citations."]),
  ("The losing side", ["It keeps the crowd: its record shows nothing of the positions, splits or dissent inside the movements counted, and it does not answer which tendency organized an event. The archive reads it as \"a holder independent of the movements and governments it counts, whose record has been used against prevailing narratives\", which \"keeps the crowd and leaves the internal argument of the movement unrecorded\". At its name the Overview put \"nonviolent\" first in a scope the consortium states without it, and left out the verification and privacy rules that bound the record."])],
 "ko": [[[2, 8, 11, 6, 7]], [[12, 13, 14]], [[16, 18, 19]], [[20, 21, 24, 25]]]},

"unionstats": {"non_row": "unionstats",
 "kernel": "The Union Membership and Coverage Database (Unionstats.com) provides private and public sector union membership, coverage and density estimates compiled from the monthly Current Population Survey by BLS methods. Barry Hirsch, David Macpherson and William Even built it; created in 2002, it is updated annually, and its unit is an estimate for a cell by year.",
 "kernel_ko": [1, 2, 3, 18],
 "sections": [
  ("What it is", ["It is \"an Internet data resource providing private and public sector labor union membership, coverage, and density estimates compiled from the monthly household Current Population Survey (CPS) using BLS methods\". Its makers are economists at Georgia State University, Trinity University and Miami University; Hirsch and Macpherson described it in a 2003 note in the ILR Review. Its estimates are compiled by academics, apart from the official series the Bureau of Labor Statistics issues as its Union Members Summary."]),
  ("What it holds", ["Economy-wide estimates begin in 1973, estimates by state, detailed industry and detailed occupation in 1983, and by metropolitan area in 1986, in historical tables running to 2025. A second section gives union wages, nonunion wages and union wage gaps, headed 1973-2022 with its one table listed 1973-2025. \"Union data compilations from the 2025 CPS completed January 23, 2026.\""]),
  ("Terms and limits", ["Its one condition is \"Use of data requires citation.\", and its one stated limit is \"CPS sample sizes are very small for some cells. Use such estimates cautiously.\" Its record settles what share of workers in a sector, state, metropolitan area, industry, occupation or demographic group were union members or covered by a contract in a year, and the union wage gap. It cannot settle \"which unions the members belong to\", \"strikes or other labor action\", or \"what members think of their unions, or any dissent or division inside them\"."]),
  ("The losing side", ["It keeps the measured extent of union membership and coverage, held by three academic economists, and records no losing side. The archive reads it \"as the measure of the base a labor organization stands on, held by independent academics outside any union; it counts members and keeps none of their positions\". At its name the Overview gave the makers, source survey and series at their grain, \"The three start years (1973, 1983, 1986) are the database's own\", and left out the citation condition and the small-cell caution."])],
 "ko": [[[1, 3, 4, 6, 7]], [[9, 10, 11]], [[15, 16, 19]], [[18, 22, 25]]]},

"gnad": {"non_row": "gnad",
 "kernel": "The Global Nonviolent Action Database (GNAD) is a database of case studies of nonviolent action campaigns, a project of Swarthmore College since 2011, developed by George Lakey with students. Its unit is the campaign, written up as a case with searchable fields and a narrative, and it counts 1,200+ cases from 100+ countries.",
 "kernel_ko": [1, 4, 5, 10, 17],
 "sections": [
  ("What it is", ["Its rule of admission is \"Campaigns, Not Movements\": the Montgomery Bus Boycott is a case, and the civil rights movement is not. It researches \"campaigns that have reached a point of completion\", sorts each into issue clusters (human rights, economic justice, democracy, national and ethnic identity, environmental sustainability, peace), and maps them by waves from \"Latin American Democracy Campaigns (1944)\" to \"Arab Awakening (2011)\". Its range runs from \"a case from ancient Egypt, 12th century BCE\" to cases \"all the way up to 2019\"."]),
  ("How it works", ["Students write its cases: seventy-six were added in one spring semester by Swarthmore's Strategy and Nonviolent Struggle course. It defines nonviolent action, after Lakey, as \"[A] technique of struggle that goes beyond institutionalized conflict procedures like law courts and voting\". Its cases are browsable by case, tag, method and map, and the public can suggest one. It is licensed CC BY-NC-ND 4.0 unless otherwise noted, and a review found its data \"freely viewable on the website\" and not available for download."]),
  ("Labor action", ["Labor cases are one part of it. Its featured cases include \"Guatemalan workers at Lunafil strike\" and \"Macalester students stage sit-in to oppose sweatshop apparel, 2000\", and its waves the \"Student Anti-Sweatshop Labor Movement (1990s - 2010s)\" and the \"Student Living Wage Movement (late 1990s - mid 2000s)\"."]),
  ("The losing side", ["It keeps the record of nonviolent campaigns written up as cases, held by Swarthmore College. Whether failed campaigns or splits inside campaigns are recorded is not shown, its \"Limitations of the Database\" page unread. The archive reads \"the case as the unit that carries a campaign's course in prose, which an event count does not\". At its name the Overview gave its host, scale, clusters and tools, and left out \"the database's own statement of its unit, limits and license\"."])],
 "ko": [[[4, 13, 14, 6, 12]], [[18, 15, 24, 23]], [[20]], [[26, 23, 7, 29, 30]]]},

"labor-notes": {"non_row": "labor-notes",
 "kernel": "Labor Notes is a media and organizing project of union activists, founded in 1979 and published by the Labor Education and Research Project: a monthly magazine, a website, books, conferences and workshops, and a network of rank-and-file members, local union leaders and labor activists. It is based in Detroit, with an East Coast office in Brooklyn.",
 "kernel_ko": [1, 2, 4],
 "sections": [
  ("What it is", ["It calls itself \"the voice of union activists who want to put the movement back in the labor movement since 1979\". It promotes \"organizing, aggressive strategies to take on employers, labor-community solidarity, and unions that are run by their members\", and it launched in the aftermath of the Bituminous Coal Strike of 1977–1978. Its monthly magazine reports news \"from workers’ point of view\", and its books include A Troublemaker’s Handbook (Dan La Botz, 1991), \"tens of thousands of these strategy manuals\" sold."]),
  ("Conferences and schools", ["Every two years it holds a national conference that \"attracts thousands of activists, about a tenth of them from other countries\". The 2022 conference brought some 4,000 to Chicago; the 2024 conference, with more than 200 meetings and workshops, was \"the largest conference we've ever had\"; the 2026 conference met June 12–14 in Chicago with 300 workshops and panels. With local unions and community groups it organizes Troublemakers Schools."]),
  ("Who holds it", ["It is an independent publication supported by subscriptions, book sales and donations, and it states the condition on which it prints criticism of leaderships: \"And as an independent publication, we’re free to include the voices who say we could be doing better—and tell how.\" Its about page judges that \"For the most part, our leaders are doing a poor job of navigating us through the crisis\"."]),
  ("Whose record, who holds it, the losing side", ["It keeps the voice of union activists and rank-and-file members, their critique of union leaderships included, and works \"to bridge the gap between isolated rank-and-file caucuses and reform groups\", Teamsters for a Democratic Union the most notable. On 31 October 2026 its home page led with stories of members contesting their own locals. Whether it keeps the record of positions after they lose is not shown.",
               "The archive reads it as \"an organ independent of the union executives it criticizes, of the kind #1725 finds holding critique: a publication, a network and a conference through which a rank-and-file position keeps being stated and can be taken up later\". At its name the composition named the caucuses it supports and left out its independence: \"the composition gives the magazine's subjects and not its standing\"."])],
 "ko": [[[1, 7, 4, 9, 23]], [[20, 21, 22, 23]], [[15, 11, 8]], [[8, 16, 18, 19], [14, 27]]]},

"labor-vector": {"non_row": "labor-vector",
 "kernel": "Labor Vector (laborvector.org) is Lee Sharks's canonical definition surface for Directionality of Semantic Labor (DSL), a layered, computable measure of the direction in which a synthetic system's labor moves relative to the task a user commissioned, under CC BY 4.0. Since 29 October 2026 it also carries a meta-aggregator of 15 resources that observe where labor and AI meet.",
 "kernel_ko": [1, 2, 7, 18],
 "sections": [
  ("What it is", ["DSL measures across five points of a transaction, \"capacity, task-origin, retrieval, output, attribution (C → T → R → O → A)\", and shows that erasure can occur at each: \"Erasure at the first layer is deepest; at the last, most visible.\" Its principle is \"Provenance is not only claim-origin. Provenance is task-origin.\" Its core is the Directionality Stack (DS-6), fixed at six places, and the site bounds its quick prompt as \"a screening estimate, not a measurement\"."]),
  ("The site and its record", ["It was deposited in the archive on 2026-06-05 as software and executable apparatus, its source recovered from the live surface on 2026-08-04. Its colophon names deposit #771, the specification of DSL, and records the page as \"drafted with Claude (TACHYON), MANUS-approved\". Its field measurement reads the Self-Audit Module's dissolution by Google AI Overview on 13 June 2026 as \"DSL measured at 1.00\"."]),
  ("The meta-aggregator", ["Its census asks one question of every record: \"who supplies the labor, who directs it, and who receives its gains — and what kind of evidence does this resource bring to that question?\" Its intersections run from labor producing AI to worker power (\"bargaining, organizing, consent, refusal, contestation and enforceable protections\"), and it publishes the census as data. It states its limits: \"A record states only what its resource measures or reports.\" and \"No record in the census measures the distribution of productivity gains between workers, employers, platforms and model providers.\""]),
  ("Whose record, who holds it, the losing side", ["It keeps the records of 15 labor-and-AI resources, held by Lee Sharks under CC BY 4.0, and records no losing side: its organizing question is who supplies, directs and receives the gains of labor. The archive reads its worker power intersection as where it meets the left census, and states \"It shares this census's editor, so it is no independent holder in #1725's sense.\" At the address \"laborvector\" on 31 October 2026 the Overview gave \"THE FRAMEWORK, ATTRIBUTED\", and the meta-aggregator was absent: \"the site is composed as its definition surface alone.\""])],
 "ko": [[[7, 10, 8, 11, 12]], [[1, 3, 4, 6, 14]], [[19, 20, 21]], [[25, 26, 29]]]},
}
